---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-osteoarthritis`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Osteoarthritis',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Osteoarthritis', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'M19', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Osteoarthritis', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Osteoarthritis: Does It Slow Joint Damage or Ease Pain?"
  description="Osteoarthritis is a wear-and-repair joint disease, not an inflammatory one. What the knee-OA trials (VIDEO, and others) actually found about vitamin D, cartilage, and pain."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-osteoarthritis", label: "Vitamin D & Osteoarthritis" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Osteoarthritis</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Osteoarthritis (OA) is the "wear-and-repair" arthritis — cartilage thins, bone remodels
      underneath, and the joint aches and stiffens. It is not the same as
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-rheumatoid-arthritis" class="text-amber-700 underline">rheumatoid arthritis</a>,
      which is autoimmune. Because vitamin D receptors sit on cartilage and bone cells, it is a
      reasonable question whether topping up vitamin D protects the joint. The trials give a fairly
      clear, and fairly deflating, answer.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The observational hint</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cohort studies produced mixed signals. Some, like early Framingham data, suggested low vitamin D
      predicted knee OA progression; others found nothing once body weight and physical activity were
      accounted for. The trouble with these studies is that people with painful knees move less and
      get outdoors less, so low vitamin D may be a <em>result</em> of the arthritis rather than a
      cause — the classic reverse-causation trap.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the randomised trials found</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Randomised trials in symptomatic knee OA have largely been negative for structure. In a widely
      cited trial (McAlindon 2013), two years of vitamin D that raised 25(OH)D above 36 ng/mL did
      <strong>not</strong> reduce cartilage volume loss or knee pain compared with placebo. A later
      Australian trial (Jin 2016, the VIDEO study) in patients with low vitamin D and knee OA similarly
      found <strong>no benefit</strong> on cartilage loss on MRI or on WOMAC pain over two years.
      Meta-analyses pooling these trials conclude any effect on pain is small and of doubtful clinical
      importance.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where a small effect might hide</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The most plausible place for a genuine benefit is <em>muscle</em>, not cartilage. Vitamin D
      deficiency causes proximal muscle weakness, and strong quadriceps protect the knee. In someone
      who is genuinely deficient, correcting that deficiency may modestly improve strength, function,
      and therefore comfort — but that is a general benefit of not being deficient, not a specific
      OA therapy. It will not regrow cartilage.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      If you have osteoarthritis and are vitamin D deficient, correcting it is worthwhile for your
      bones and muscles — but do not expect it to halt joint damage or replace the interventions that
      actually work: weight management (each kilogram lost meaningfully unloads the knee), structured
      exercise and strengthening, and appropriate analgesia. Vitamin D is supportive background, not
      a disease-modifying treatment for OA.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Persistent joint pain deserves a proper diagnosis —
      osteoarthritis, inflammatory arthritis, and osteomalacia can feel similar but are treated very
      differently. Correct a genuine deficiency, but pursue the exercise and weight strategies that
      have the real evidence.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-osteoarthritis" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
